            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Angels, heavenly beings, or spiritual authorities (both faithful and fallen), particularly in prophetic and apocalyptic literature.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Revelation 1:20\",\"Revelation 12:4\",\"Job 38:7\",\"Daniel 8:10\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The innumerable multitude of God's covenant people or Abraham's descendants.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 15:5\",\"Genesis 22:17\",\"Nehemiah 9:23\"],\"prominence\":\"dominant\"},{\"meaning\":\"Glorified saints or righteous believers reflecting God's truth and light in a dark world.\",\"basis\":[\"Daniel 12:3\",\"Philippians 2:15\",\"1 Corinthians 15:41-42\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The Messiah or Jesus Christ (specifically identified as a star or the 'Morning Star').\",\"basis\":[\"Numbers 24:17\",\"Revelation 22:16\",\"2 Peter 1:19\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Earthly patriarchs, leaders, or the twelve tribes of Israel.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 37:9\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbolism of stars is polyvalent and highly dependent on biblical genre, though interpretations within specific contexts are well-settled. In apocalyptic literature, stars predominantly symbolize angels, demons, or cosmic spiritual powers. In patriarchal literature, they function as a metaphor for numerical vastness. Singularly, a star (especially the Morning Star) is a widely recognized Messianic symbol.\"}",
